Subproblem 1: Identify the groups of features that best capture software module size, complexity, and effort/difficulty metrics, along with any density or interaction-based indicators.
Answer 1: To capture software module size, complexity, effort/difficulty metrics, and density/interaction-based indicators, the features can be grouped as follows:

### 1. Size-related Metrics:
   - loc (lines of code)
   - log_loc (log-transformed loc)
   - n (Halstead total operators + operands)
   - lOCode (Halstead's line count)
   - total_Op (total operators)
   - total_Opnd (total operands)
   - total_operators_operands (sum of total operators and operands)

---

### 2. Complexity Metrics:
   - v(g) (cyclomatic complexity)
   - ev(g) (essential complexity)
   - iv(g) (design complexity)
   - overall_complexity (sum of v(g), ev(g), iv(g))
   - branchCount (number of branches)
   - v_g_per_loc (cyclomatic complexity per loc)
   - branch_and_complexity_interaction (interaction of branches and complexity)

---

### 3. Effort/Difficulty Metrics:
   - e (Halstead effort)
   - log_e (log-transformed Halstead effort)
   - d (Halstead difficulty)
   - i (Halstead intelligence)
   - l (Halstead program length)
   - size_weighted_difficulty (module size × difficulty)
   - effort_density (effort per line of code)
   - difficulty_per_complexity (difficulty per cyclomatic complexity)
   - effort_per_complexity (effort per cyclomatic complexity)

---

### 4. Density & Interaction-based Indicators:
   - branch_density (branch count per loc)
   - volume_per_decision_point (Halstead volume per cyclomatic complexity)
   - uniq_op_to_total_op_ratio (ratio of unique operators to total operators)
   - loc_to_comment_ratio (ratio of lines of code to lines of comments)

These groups provide a structured way to represent different attributes of the software module across size, complexity, effort/difficulty, and density/interaction properties. This categorization will assist in further downstream modeling and feature generation.

